{
  "image_name": "461b0617e67bdbf330d79b2d06617258.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the long-term carbon cycle and compares a simplified “textbook” view with a more complete “emerging” view. Both versions show carbon moving among the atmosphere, land, ocean, sediments, and Earth’s interior through weathering, burial, subduction, and volcanic degassing. In the textbook view, the cycle is dominated by inorganic pathways: silicate weathering on land consumes atmospheric carbon, carbonate burial stores it in marine sediments, and subduction plus volcanism return carbon to the surface. Carbon stocks such as calcium carbonate and rock organic carbon are also indicated.\n\nThe emerging view adds major organic-carbon and sulfur-related pathways, making the cycle more complex. It highlights erosion of biospheric organic carbon, burial of organic carbon in sediments, and oxidation of rock organic carbon, all of which contribute substantially to carbon exchange. It also includes sulfide oxidation and carbonate weathering as an additional flux. The main message is that Earth’s carbon budget is not controlled only by silicate weathering and volcanic outgassing; organic carbon cycling and associated oxidative weathering appear to be comparably important parts of the system. Colored labels distinguish inorganic fluxes, organic fluxes, and long-term carbon reservoirs.",
